title: init stamps the encoding marker on a half-board, cutting off its migration
updated: 2026-08-11T20:30:27Z
---

Init.pm:79 refuses only when board_exists (which is exactly refs/karr/config,
BoardStore.pm:38-41). A half-board -- task refs present, config ref missing --
therefore passes that gate, and Init.pm:98 then calls stamp_encoding_version
unconditionally. That writes refs/karr/meta/encoding, whose meaning is "this
board was written under the current character-encoding contract".

On a half-board carrying pre-0.403 payloads that claim is false. Consequence:
board_is_legacy_encoded turns 0, the mojibake repair in the read path is
skipped, and every old task suddenly reads wrong:

    before:  DBD::mysql als Transport pruefen — DBD::MariaDB verbindet nicht
    after:   DBD::mysql als Transport pruefen a  DBD::MariaDB verbindet nicht

The bytes in the refs are untouched; it is the read path that stops repairing.
Worse, `karr repair` now reports the board as up to date (Repair.pm:101), so the
one command that could still fix it declines to run. The board is left readable
only through a hand-deleted marker ref.

Found for real: an agent hit "No karr board found" on a repo holding 21 tickets
(see the sibling ticket about that message), ran karr init as the message told
it to, and silently lost correct reading of every old ticket.

init must not stamp when the board it is completing may carry legacy payloads.
Stamping is only truthful for a genuinely fresh board -- i.e. when nothing lives
under refs/karr/ at all. When init completes a half-board, leave the marker
absent so the read path keeps repairing and `karr repair --yes` stays available.

Recovery path that works today, for reference: delete refs/karr/meta/encoding,
then karr repair (dry run), then karr repair --yes.
